    "description": "The National Iranian Gas Company (NIGC) is a state-owned organization associated with natural gas processing, transmission, and distribution in Iran. Its network functions are institutionally distinct from the upstream field-development responsibilities described for NIOC. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["NIGC", "Natural gas", "Gas transmission", "Gas distribution", "Energy infrastructure"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:05:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:05:55Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "National Iranian Gas Company: Processing and Networks | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to NIGC’s gas-network responsibilities, state ownership, and the evidence needed to assess delivery performance."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "network-role",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From gas resources to delivered fuel",
        "content": "The National Iranian Gas Company, abbreviated to NIGC, occupies the processing and delivery side of Iran’s natural gas industry. The EIA’s institutional reference assigns it downstream gas activities, including processing and domestic distribution. This distinguishes its functions from NIOC’s upstream responsibilities for oil and gas fields. [s1]\n\nThe distinction is useful when reading reports about new projects. Developing a field and delivering usable gas through a network are different activities. An increase in resources available underground does not, by itself, demonstrate increased delivery to customers. The institutional division described by the EIA is a guide to these functions rather than proof of the current ownership of every facility. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ownership-and-legal-description",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "State ownership and corporate identity",
        "content": "Global Energy Monitor’s ownership profile identifies NIGC as an Iranian legal entity headquartered in Tehran. It records the Ministry of Petroleum as its direct owner and the Government of Iran as its ultimate owner, and describes the entity as not publicly listed. This provides an ownership reference independent of the company’s own promotional material, although it is not a substitute for a current corporate registry extract. [s2]\n\nA separate historical source is Iran’s August 2020 reply in the International Court of Justice case concerning Certain Iranian Assets. The filing describes NIGC as a state-owned public company with independent juridical status and cites its articles of association. It identifies activities including gas refining, domestic supply, exports, and supervision of the distribution network. These are representations made by Iran in litigation, not findings that the Court necessarily adopted. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "seasonal-system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why seasonal demand matters",
        "content": "The EIA’s 2024 assessment explains that residential and commercial gas use rises in winter, while electricity-sector demand increases in summer. It identifies inadequate storage for variable seasonal demand as a system constraint. These national conditions help explain the importance of transmission and storage, but do not independently establish NIGC’s efficiency or responsibility for any particular interruption. [s4]\n\nEvaluating network performance requires observations with clear boundaries: gas entering transmission, gas delivered to customers, and losses cannot be assumed to measure the same thing. A current assessment should establish those definitions before comparing annual totals or reporting claims of record daily delivery.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
