    "description": "The National Iranian Oil Refining & Distribution Company (NIORDC) is a state-owned organization associated with Iran’s petroleum refining and fuel-distribution system. Its documented responsibilities connect refining infrastructure with storage, petroleum pipelines, and delivery of products to the domestic market. [s1]",
    "tags": ["NIORDC", "Oil refining", "Fuel distribution", "Petroleum products", "Energy infrastructure"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:05:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:05:55Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "NIORDC: Iran’s Petroleum Refining and Distribution Functions | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand NIORDC’s documented responsibilities and why refining capacity, fuel output, and domestic supply require separate evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "institutional-role",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connecting refineries with fuel users",
        "content": "The National Iranian Oil Refining & Distribution Company, abbreviated to NIORDC, belongs to the downstream petroleum system. The EIA’s institutional reference describes responsibilities covering refining and petroleum-product distribution, including refinery and storage construction, pipelines, and gasoline-station operations. [s1]\n\nThis remit differs from NIOC’s upstream field-development role and NIGC’s gas-processing and delivery functions. It concerns the infrastructure through which petroleum becomes usable fuel and reaches consumers. The functional description does not establish that NIORDC directly owns every refinery, depot, or retail outlet; a current ownership map requires facility-specific evidence. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "capacity-and-supply",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Refining capacity is not finished-fuel supply",
        "content": "The EIA’s October 2024 assessment reports continuing gasoline imports despite expansion of Iran’s processing capacity, linking the situation to rising demand and refinery limitations. It also records the addition of a new distillation unit at Abadan in March 2023. These are national refining developments, not a consolidated measure of NIORDC’s own output or profitability. [s2]\n\nThe distinction matters when assessing investment announcements: additional processing capacity does not automatically establish an equivalent increase in gasoline available to customers. A reliable account must identify the facility, the kind of capacity being measured, and whether a reported addition has actually entered operation.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "project-outlooks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading development schedules carefully",
        "content": "A February 2025 refining outlook published by the Oxford Institute for Energy Studies lists South Adish and an additional Persian Gulf Star train among Iranian condensate-splitting projects associated with NIORDC. Condensate is a liquid hydrocarbon associated with gas production; it is distinguished from crude oil in refinery statistics. The outlook’s project schedule is evidence of expectations at publication, rather than confirmation of completion by the dates shown. [s3]\n\nThe EIA’s earlier assessment also describes refinery projects under construction and their expected future start-ups. Neither publication establishes their operating status at this page’s October 2026 cutoff. A current profile therefore needs commissioning records and actual throughput before presenting those projects as completed additions. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a complete company assessment needs",
        "content": "NIORDC’s documented scope makes both production infrastructure and distribution infrastructure relevant to its profile. [s1] Assessing performance requires more than a list of refineries: it needs verified ownership and operating relationships, delivered product volumes, product-quality information, and evidence about transport and storage. Those items remain research requirements here rather than implied achievements.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
